    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "43481be6-03be-562b-89a5-7c8695090e89",
      ":question/problem": "Find ${y}^{\\prime}$ for $y=\\frac{\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}}{e^{3 x}}.$\n\n| ${y}^{\\prime}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $x e^{3 x}$ |",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}$ and the denominator $v(x)=e^{3 x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\quad v'(x)=3 e^{3 x}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\\prime} & =\\frac{u'(x)v(x)-u(x)v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2} \\\\ & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})(e^{3 x})-(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})(3 e^{3 x})}{(e^{3 x})^2} \\\\ & =- \\frac{\\left(3 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1\\right) e^{- 3 x}}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "f9ca27b6-6a71-5e3d-abb9-6924714b214c",
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
          ":answer-field/correct": [
